 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 17,
   "y": 12,
   "text": "LANCASTER CASTLE. A fortress above the River Lune for nearly a thousand years, and a working prison until 2011. The Pendle witches were tried here in 1612, a sad chapter. The tour guides have a joke about the stairs."
  },
  {
   "x": 13,
   "y": 14,
   "text": "THE PRIORY. The parish church stands on Roman and Saxon ground, next door to the castle, which is the sort of arrangement you get when a town has been busy for two thousand years."
  },
  {
   "x": 45,
   "y": 18,
   "text": "THE LANCASTER CANAL. Opened in 1797, with no locks for most of its length. Barges carried coal and limestone to Kendal. Now they carry holidaymakers and sandwiches. The towpath is flat, which is the canal's nicest feature."
  },
  {
   "x": 31,
   "y": 34,
   "text": "THE LUNE. A fast, wide river that gave Lancaster its name (the Roman fort on the Lune). Ships once sailed up it to the Old Port, full of sugar and rum and cotton. The quay has been quiet for a century, which makes it a good place to sit and eat chips."
  },
  {
   "x": 12,
   "y": 34,
   "text": "MILLENNIUM BRIDGE. Opened in 2001, a footbridge shaped like a sail. It's the best place to watch the tide come in at dusk. Take your time. Everyone in Lancaster does."
  },
  {
   "x": 38,
   "y": 7,
   "text": "BLUE PLAQUE: The Pendle witches were tried at Lancaster Castle in August 1612.",
   "kind": "plaque",
   "dx": -0.475,
   "plaque": "lancaster_pendle_witches",
   "title": "The Pendle Witches"
  },
  {
   "x": 10,
   "y": 13,
   "plaque": "lancaster_roman_rider",
   "people": "romans",
   "title": "The Roman Rider",
   "text": "In 2005 builders in Lancaster dug up a Roman tombstone carved with a cavalryman on horseback, holding up the severed head of a beaten enemy. He was Insus, a horseman from Gaul, who served at the fort that once stood on Castle Hill. Grim, but beautifully carved."
  },
  {
   "x": 26,
   "y": 28,
   "plaque": "lancaster_captured_africans",
   "people": "black_britain",
   "title": "Captured Africans",
   "text": "In the 1700s Lancaster was Britain's fourth busiest slave-trading port, after Liverpool, London and Bristol. Ships left St George's Quay for West Africa and the Caribbean, and the town grew rich on the sugar and mahogany that came back. A memorial on the quay now remembers the people who were taken."
  },
  {
   "x": 40,
   "y": 19,
   "plaque": "lancaster_lino_king",
   "title": "The Lino King",
   "text": "For generations Lancaster was a lino town. James Williamson's works made linoleum and oilcloth on a huge scale, and his son, Lord Ashton, the man behind the Ashton Memorial, became known as the Lino King. When the wind was right, the whole town smelled of linseed oil."
  },
  {
   "x": 40,
   "y": 33,
   "plaque": "lancaster_bay_birds",
   "title": "Morecambe Bay Birds",
   "text": "Morecambe Bay is the biggest stretch of intertidal mud and sand in Britain, and in winter it feeds huge flocks of knot, dunlin, oystercatchers and curlews. When the tide pushes them up the shore, thousands wheel round together like smoke. Bring binoculars and a flask."
  }
 ],
